a","identifier":"https://ror.org/02qa1x782"}],"order":4},{"firstName":"Paul","lastName":"Bentzen","email":"paul.bentzen@dal.ca","affiliation":"Dalhousie University","affiliationROR":"https://ror.org/01e6qks80","affiliations":[{"organization":"Dalhousie University","identifier":"https://ror.org/01e6qks80"}],"orcid":"0000-0003-4288-7730","order":5},{"firstName":"Anne","lastName":"Dalziel","email":"anne.dalziel@smu.ca","affiliation":"Saint Mary's University","affiliationROR":"https://ror.org/010zh7098","affiliations":[{"organization":"Saint Mary's University","identifier":"https://ror.org/010zh7098"}],"orcid":"0000-0002-3032-4714","order":6}],"abstract":"\u003cp\u003eMany fish populations that would normally migrate between fresh water and the ocean have become landlocked after river damming. To restore migration, these populations must retain the physiological capacity to tolerate life in the ocean, including tolerance to high salinities. The Atlantic Whitefish (\u003cem\u003eCoregonus huntsmani\u003c/em\u003e) is an endangered, ancestrally anadromous fish that persists as a landlocked form in a single Canadian watershed. To assess the potential for restoring anadromy in this species as part of their recovery plan, we examined the effects of salinity on F\u003csub\u003e1\u003c/sub\u003e captive-bred juveniles reared at 0, 15, and 30 ppt over 10 months. We measured growth, condition, and sub-organismal indicators of metabolism (blood glucose, lactate), osmoregulation (plasma osmolarity), and stress (cortisol) in 13-month-old fish under cool (12  ±  2 °C) and 16-month-old fish in warm (17  ±  2 °C) conditions. The data included herein was used to analyze all measures of performance across salinities and temperatures. \u003c/p\u003e\n","funders":[{"organization":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","identifierType":"ror","identifier":"https://ror.org/01h531d29","awardNumber":"2024-04053","awardDescription":"","awardTitle":"Discovery Grant ","orde